::GEO_U03_L07_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.2
Question:
In a parallelogram, which sides are congruent?
{
~A. Adjacent sides only#Adjacent sides are not always congruent in every parallelogram.
=B. Opposite sides#Opposite sides of a parallelogram are congruent.
~C. Diagonals only#Diagonals have other relationships but are not side pairs.
~D. No sides#Parallelograms do have congruent opposite sides.
}
::GEO_U03_L07_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.2
Question:
In a parallelogram, which angles are congruent?
{
~A. Consecutive angles#Consecutive angles are supplementary, not necessarily congruent.
~B. All exterior angles only#Exterior angles are not the standard relationship used here.
~C. No angles#Parallelograms do have congruent opposite angles.
=D. Opposite angles#Opposite angles of a parallelogram are congruent.
}
::GEO_U03_L07_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.2
Question:
In a parallelogram, consecutive angles are what?
{
=A. Supplementary#Consecutive angles in a parallelogram sum to 180°.
~B. Always congruent#Opposite angles are congruent; consecutive angles are supplementary.
~C. Always 90°#Only rectangles guarantee right angles.
~D. Always acute#Consecutive angles can vary but must sum to 180°.
}
::GEO_U03_L07_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.2
Question:
What do the diagonals of a parallelogram do?
{
~A. Stay perpendicular in every case#Perpendicular diagonals are special, not true for every parallelogram.
~B. Have no relationship#There is a defined diagonal relationship.
=C. Bisect each other#Diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other.
~D. Create four equilateral triangles#This is not a general parallelogram property.
}
::GEO_U03_L07_Q05::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.2
Question:
If one angle of a parallelogram is 65°, what is the measure of a consecutive angle?
{
~A. 65°#That is the opposite angle, not a consecutive angle.
=B. 115°#Consecutive angles are supplementary, so 180 - 65 \= 115.
~C. 90°#A parallelogram is not necessarily a rectangle.
~D. 180°#180° is the sum, not the angle measure.
}
::GEO_U03_L07_Q06::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.3
Question:
In an isosceles trapezoid, which angles are congruent?
{
=A. Base angles on the same base#Base angles of an isosceles trapezoid are congruent.
~B. Consecutive interior angles only#Consecutive interior angles along a leg are supplementary.
~C. All four angles always#All four angles are not always congruent.
~D. No angles#There is a base-angle relationship.
}
::GEO_U03_L07_Q07::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.3
Question:
The parallel sides of a trapezoid are called what?
{
~A. Legs#Legs are the non-parallel sides.
~B. Diagonals#Diagonals connect nonadjacent vertices.
~C. Medians only#A median or midsegment is different from a base.
=D. Bases#The parallel sides of a trapezoid are the bases.
}
::GEO_U03_L07_Q08::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.3
Question:
The non-parallel sides of a trapezoid are called what?
{
~A. Bases#Bases are the parallel sides.
~B. Diagonals#Diagonals connect nonadjacent vertices.
=C. Legs#The non-parallel sides are the legs.
~D. Opposite bases#Opposite bases is not the name of the non-parallel sides.
}
::GEO_U03_L07_Q09::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.3
Question:
The trapezoid midsegment length equals what?
{
=A. The average of the two base lengths#A trapezoid midsegment length is the average of the bases.
~B. The sum of the legs#Leg lengths are not averaged for the midsegment formula.
~C. Half of one base only#Both bases are used.
~D. The product of the bases#The formula uses an average, not a product.
}
::GEO_U03_L07_Q10::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.3
Question:
A trapezoid has bases 8 and 14. What is the midsegment length?
{
~A. 6#This subtracts the bases instead of averaging them.
~B. 22#This adds the bases but does not divide by 2.
~C. 112#This multiplies the bases.
=D. 11#The midsegment is (8 + 14) / 2 \= 11.
}
::GEO_U03_L07_Q11::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.2
Question:
A parallelogram has one side of length 12. What is true about the opposite side?
{
~A. It is 6.#There is no halving relationship.
=B. It is also 12.#Opposite sides of a parallelogram are congruent.
~C. It is 24.#There is no doubling relationship.
~D. It cannot be related.#The opposite side relationship is known.
}
::GEO_U03_L07_Q12::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.2
Question:
A parallelogram has diagonals that intersect at M. If AM \= 7 on diagonal AC, what is MC?
{
~A. 14#14 would be the whole diagonal AC, not MC.
~B. 3.5#3.5 incorrectly halves AM.
=C. 7#Diagonals bisect each other, so AM \= MC.
~D. Cannot be known#The bisection property gives the value.
}
::GEO_U03_L07_Q13::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.2
Question:
Which condition is enough to identify a parallelogram?
{
~A. Exactly one pair of opposite sides is parallel.#One pair of parallel sides describes trapezoid-related evidence, not necessarily a parallelogram.
~B. Only one angle is acute.#Angle type alone is not enough.
~C. The figure has four sides only.#A four-sided figure is any quadrilateral.
=D. Both pairs of opposite sides are parallel.#A parallelogram has both pairs of opposite sides parallel.
}
::GEO_U03_L07_Q14::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.3
Question:
Which statement about an isosceles trapezoid is true?
{
=A. Its legs are congruent.#An isosceles trapezoid has congruent legs.
~B. Its bases are always congruent.#Bases are parallel but not necessarily congruent.
~C. All sides are congruent.#All sides congruent is not the trapezoid relationship.
~D. It has no base angles.#Trapezoids have base angles.
}
::GEO_U03_L07_Q15::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.3
Question:
If a trapezoid midsegment is 15 and one base is 12, what is the other base?
{
~A. 3#This subtracts incorrectly.
~B. 27#This adds 12 and 15 without using the average formula.
=C. 18#(12 + x) / 2 \= 15, so 12 + x \= 30 and x \= 18.
~D. 30#This is twice the midsegment, not the missing base.
}
::GEO_U03_L07_Q16::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.2
Question:
In a parallelogram, if one angle is 110°, what is the opposite angle?
{
~A. 70°#70° is a consecutive angle.
=B. 110°#Opposite angles in a parallelogram are congruent.
~C. 90°#A parallelogram is not necessarily a rectangle.
~D. 180°#180° is the supplementary total for consecutive angles.
}
::GEO_U03_L07_Q17::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.2
Question:
Which property helps prove two triangles inside a parallelogram congruent using a diagonal?
{
~A. All sides are different.#Parallelograms have opposite side relationships.
~B. Consecutive angles are always equal.#Consecutive angles are supplementary, not always equal.
=C. Opposite sides are congruent and the diagonal is shared.#Opposite side congruence and a shared diagonal can support triangle congruence.
~D. The diagonal is not part of either triangle.#A diagonal can be a shared side of two triangles.
}
::GEO_U03_L07_Q18::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.3
Question:
In an isosceles trapezoid, if one base angle is 72°, what is the other angle on the same base?
{
=A. 72°#Base angles on the same base of an isosceles trapezoid are congruent.
~B. 108°#108° may be adjacent along a leg, not the same-base angle.
~C. 36°#There is no halving relationship.
~D. 144°#There is no doubling relationship.
}
::GEO_U03_L07_Q19::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.2
Question:
Which quadrilateral property is specific to parallelograms in this lesson?
{
~A. Only one pair of sides is parallel.#One pair of parallel sides is trapezoid-related.
~B. Base angles are always congruent.#Base angles are a key isosceles trapezoid relationship.
~C. Midsegment equals average of bases.#The midsegment formula is for trapezoids.
=D. Diagonals bisect each other.#Diagonals bisect each other in parallelograms.
}
::GEO_U03_L07_Q20::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.3
Question:
Which relationship belongs to trapezoids?
{
~A. Opposite sides are always congruent.#Opposite sides congruent is a parallelogram property.
=B. Midsegment length equals the average of the bases.#The trapezoid midsegment uses the average of the bases.
~C. Diagonals always bisect each other.#Diagonal bisection is a parallelogram property.
~D. Consecutive angles are always 90°.#Consecutive right angles are not guaranteed.
}
::GEO_U03_L07_Q21::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.2
Question:
Which first step best supports this lesson skill?
{
~A. Guess from the picture before checking labels.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
=B. Identify the givens and the geometric object before choosing a relationship.#This is correct because it begins with the lesson evidence and connects the work to Quadrilateral Relationships and Proofs.
~C. Skip directly to the final answer.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
~D. Use a formula that is not connected to the given information.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
}
::GEO_U03_L07_Q22::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.2
Question:
Which explanation best shows mastery of this lesson?
{
~A. The answer only states a number.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
~B. The answer says the diagram looks correct.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
~C. The answer copies vocabulary without using it.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
=D. The answer names the evidence, applies the correct relationship, and interprets the conclusion.#This is correct because it begins with the lesson evidence and connects the work to Quadrilateral Relationships and Proofs.
}
::GEO_U03_L07_Q23::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.2
Question:
What should a student check before submitting work for this lesson?
{
=A. That labels, notation, units, and reasoning match the lesson relationship.#This is correct because it begins with the lesson evidence and connects the work to Quadrilateral Relationships and Proofs.
~B. That every answer is longer than the choices.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
~C. That no diagram or evidence is included.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
~D. That the work uses a future unit topic.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
}
::GEO_U03_L07_Q24::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.2
Question:
Which error should be avoided in this lesson?
{
~A. Writing the given information first.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
~B. Explaining why a theorem applies.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
=C. Using appearance or an unrelated formula instead of the evidence in the task.#This is correct because it begins with the lesson evidence and connects the work to Quadrilateral Relationships and Proofs.
~D. Checking the final answer in context.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
}
::GEO_U03_L07_Q25::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.2
Question:
Which response is most aligned to the lesson objective?
{
~A. A response using a topic from a later unit.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
=B. A step-by-step solution for Quadrilateral Relationships and Proofs that cites the correct evidence.#This is correct because it begins with the lesson evidence and connects the work to Quadrilateral Relationships and Proofs.
~C. A response with no labels or explanation.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
~D. A response that changes the given information.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
}
